  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Acoustic gems and a soaring Morning Dew close the show",
    "blurb": "The first set features an intimate acoustic segment with folk‑gospel numbers and a rare Everly Brothers cover. The electric second set builds energy toward a powerful rendition of Morning Dew, highlighted by Jerry’s pedal‑steel and lead guitar work. Good Lovin' showcases Bob’s vocals while Pigpen adds a brief keyboard solo.",
    "review": "On July 11 1970 at the Fillmore East the Dead delivered a show that feels like a snapshot of an era in transition. Set 1 is a low‑key, acoustic showcase – Jerry slipping a pedal‑steel into “Swing Low (Sweet Chariot)” and “So Sad (To Watch Good Love Go Bad)”, Pigpen quietly adding a keyboard solo, and the band weaving gospel‑inflected folk tunes that are rare on any recording. The second set erupts with the classic early‑70s electric punch: “Morning Dew” soars with raw intensity, punctuated by Jerry’s heavy lead guitar tones (early wah‑wah hints) and Bob’s vocal fire on a double “Good Lovin’”. Though captured only on an audience tape that loses the right channel for the first half, the performance’s historic rarity and musical highs make it worth the listen.",
